window.EG=window.EG||{series:{},episodes:{}};
EG.long=EG.long||{};
Object.assign(EG.long,{
"col-42867":{"s":"Dr Ray Flemming is a polished Los Angeles psychiatrist with a wealthy wife, a thriving practice and a secret: his lover, the young actress Joan Hudson. When his wife becomes an obstacle, he plans her death with a clinician's care, building an alibi that depends on Joan playing a part for him far from the scene.\n\nInto this tidy arrangement walks a rumpled police lieutenant in a shabby raincoat, apologising for taking up the doctor's time. Flemming barely notices him at first. He soon should, because the lieutenant keeps noticing small things that do not quite add up, and he keeps coming back with just one more question.","x":"Columbo cannot prove his theory, so he sets a trap. He stages what looks like Joan's suicide and lets her overhear him mocking her, which turns her against Flemming. She agrees to testify, and the doctor's alibi collapses.","n":"This pilot was directed by Richard Irving and adapted by Richard Levinson and William Link from their own stage play. Gene Barry plays the doctor, with Nina Foch and Katherine Justice in the cast. It aired in February 1968 and established the series' pattern of showing the crime first."},
"col-42868":{"s":"Leslie Williams is a sharp, successful lawyer who has outgrown her marriage and has no wish to share her money with a husband she finds dull. She devises a plan that is as cold as it is clever: a kidnapping that never really happened, complete with a ransom demand and a worried public performance for the authorities.\n\nThe FBI arrives and takes the situation at face value. Lieutenant Columbo, however, is more interested in the odd details than in the official story. He chats, wanders around the house and politely refuses to stay out of the way.","x":"Columbo persuades the dead man's young stepdaughter to demand money from Leslie. When Leslie pays her from the ransom cash, the marked serial numbers connect her to the supposed kidnapping and give Columbo the proof he needs.","n":"The second pilot was directed by Richard Irving, from a story by Levinson and Link with a teleplay by Dean Hargrove. Lee Grant stars as the lawyer, Harlan Warde plays her husband and Patricia Mattick the stepdaughter. It aired in March 1971, three years after the first pilot."},
"col-42800":{"s":"Ken Franklin and Jim Ferris are the famous writing partnership behind a string of bestselling detective novels. The truth is that Jim does all the real work, while Ken takes the credit and the royalties. When Jim announces that he intends to carry on alone, Ken sees his comfortable life about to disappear.\n\nHe decides that the perfect crime is a matter of plotting, and he plots carefully, with a telephone call as the centrepiece of his alibi. Then a crumpled lieutenant arrives, full of admiration for the writer's books and very curious about how the plots are put together.","x":"Columbo discovers that Jim habitually jotted story ideas on any scrap of paper nearby. He finds a note describing the very phone-call alibi Ken used, and Ken is forced to confess.","n":"Directed by a young Steven Spielberg and written by Steven Bochco, this is one of the best-loved episodes. Jack Cassidy plays the killer, the first of his three Columbo villains. TV Guide ranked it among the greatest episodes of the series."},
"col-42801":{"s":"Brimmer runs a private investigation agency and has a reputation for discretion. When a wealthy publisher hires him to follow his wife, Brimmer sees an opportunity and tries to blackmail her over what he finds. She refuses to be frightened and threatens to expose him instead.\n\nThe argument ends in a moment of violence, and Brimmer is left with a body and very little time. He is a professional, and he knows exactly how a detective would examine a scene like this. What he has not planned for is a detective who looks nothing like the ones he has met.","x":"Columbo matches a cut on the victim's cheek to a ring Brimmer wears. He then invents a story about a lost contact lens, and Brimmer, anxious to check his car boot, walks into the trap and confesses.","n":"Bernard Kowalski directed and Levinson and Link wrote this episode, which won an Emmy for its writing. Robert Culp plays the killer and returned to the series as a murderer twice more. Ray Milland and Patricia Crowley also appear."},
"col-42802":{"s":"Martin Hollister is a retired Marine general, decorated, respected and now quietly in financial trouble. When a business partner threatens to bring that trouble into the open, Hollister does not hesitate. There is a gunshot, and there is one witness: a young divorcée named Helen Stewart, who saw more than the general would like.\n\nHollister is charming and persuasive, and he sets about convincing her that she is mistaken about what she saw. Columbo, meanwhile, becomes more interested in the general's story than the general would like, and he starts asking about a certain old revolver.","x":"A remark from the cook makes Columbo realise that Hollister would never have lost his signature revolver, as he claims. Forensics then show that the gun is the murder weapon, which destroys the general's account.","n":"Jack Smight directed this episode from a script by John T. Dugan. Eddie Albert plays the general and Suzanne Pleshette plays the witness, with Kate Reid as her mother. It was shown in October 1971, in the first regular season."},
"col-42803":{"s":"Dale Kingston is an art critic with a fine eye, expensive tastes and a rich uncle whose private collection would solve all his problems. When the uncle is found dead and several paintings are missing, everything points to a burglary that ended badly.\n\nBut Kingston has done more than steal. He has arranged for suspicion to fall on someone else, and he has prepared an alibi that looks sound from every angle. Lieutenant Columbo is polite, humble and apparently confused, but he has noticed that this particular tragedy seems a little too well organised.","x":"Kingston hides the stolen paintings so that the police will find them. Columbo's fingerprints appear on the canvases from an earlier visit, but he had been wearing gloves, which proves Kingston's story false.","n":"Hy Averback directed this episode and Jackson Gillis wrote it. Ross Martin, known as a master of disguise in the television series The Wild Wild West, plays the art critic. Don Ameche and Kim Hunter appear in supporting roles."},
"col-42804":{"s":"Beth Chadwick has spent her life in the shadow of her domineering brother, who controls the family and its money. When he threatens to ruin the man she loves, something finally breaks, and she plans to deal with him in a way that looks like an accident.\n\nBeth intends to pass the shooting off as an unfortunate mistake, as if she had taken her brother for a burglar. Her story is simple, her alibi is respectable and her grief is convincing. Columbo listens kindly, but he has already begun to wonder about small details of the night in question.","x":"Columbo checks the timeline and finds that the testimony of Beth's boyfriend contradicts her account. The gates and the alarm do not fit her story, and her careful alibi falls apart.","n":"Norman Lloyd, the actor and producer, directed this episode, working from a story by Barney Slater and a teleplay by Steven Bochco. Susan Clark plays Beth, and Leslie Nielsen and Richard Anderson appear. It was the final screen role of Jessie Royce Landis."},
"col-42805":{"s":"Roger Stanford is the gifted young chemist at the heart of a family chemical company, and he feels the business should be his. His uncle, who runs it, thinks otherwise and holds a secret that could ruin Roger. So Roger puts his scientific talent to work and prepares a gift that will solve both problems at once.\n\nWhen the explosion comes, it looks like a dreadful accident. Roger is calm, helpful and even grieving, and he is eager to see the company's future settled. Columbo, in his usual untidy style, wonders whether accidents are ever quite so convenient.","x":"On a San Francisco cable car, Columbo lets Roger believe that a damaged cigar box was recovered from the wreckage. Roger panics, and the reaction shows he knows what the real box contained.","n":"Edward M. Abroms directed, with a story by Lester and Tina Pine and Jackson Gillis and a teleplay by Gillis. Roddy McDowall plays the young chemist, and Ida Lupino and Anne Francis appear. The episode makes striking use of San Francisco locations."},
"col-42806":{"s":"Elliot Markham is a brilliant architect with a bold design and a big problem: the money for his dream project comes from a wealthy Texan who is losing patience. When the millionaire threatens to withdraw his support, Markham decides that a disappearance would suit everyone, himself above all.\n\nHe hides the body and lets the world believe that his backer has simply gone abroad again. He is smooth, confident and apparently helpful. Columbo, however, is curious about the missing man and spends a surprising amount of time studying the building site and the man's possessions.","x":"Markham keeps urging Columbo to dig on the site, and then moves the body when nothing is found. Columbo, who had noticed that the dead man's car radio was tuned to the wrong sort of music, is waiting for him.","n":"This is the only episode of the series that Peter Falk himself directed. The story is by William Kelley, with a teleplay by Steven Bochco. Patrick O'Neal plays the architect, and Forrest Tucker and John Fiedler appear."},
"col-42807":{"s":"Alex Benedict is one of the most admired conductors in the country, with a devoted public and a respectable marriage. He is also having an affair with a gifted young pianist whose ambitions and demands have begun to threaten the life he has built so carefully.\n\nWhen she dies, the scene suggests a sad suicide, and Benedict plays the role of the shocked, grieving colleague with great skill. Lieutenant Columbo is no musician, but he is a patient listener, and he has noticed that something about the performance does not quite fit.","x":"Benedict returns to the scene to retrieve a lost boutonnière. Television footage shows him leaving the house wearing it, while a recording of the concert shows he was not, and his wife will not support his alibi.","n":"Nicholas Colasanto, later known as Coach in Cheers, directed this episode. The story is by Levinson and Link and the teleplay by Steven Bochco. John Cassavetes plays the conductor, and Blythe Danner, Myrna Loy and Pat Morita appear."},
"col-42808":{"s":"What begins as a kidnapping case soon takes a darker turn. A young man has apparently been abducted, and a ransom is demanded from his wealthy family. Columbo arrives to find that the situation involves a trust fund, a squabbling family and one very devoted collector of orchids.\n\nHis uncle manages the money with great care, and with a love for his greenhouse that seems almost excessive. As the crime becomes more serious, the lieutenant finds himself in a damp, glass-walled world of rare flowers and lethal secrets, with a new and unwanted police partner at his side.","x":"Columbo uses a metal detector in the greenhouse and finds an old bullet. It matches the gun that was planted at the house of the victim's wife, which links the crime to the uncle.","n":"Boris Sagal directed this episode, written by Jonathan Latimer. Ray Milland plays the orchid-loving uncle, and Bradford Dillman plays his nephew. Bob Dishy appears as Sergeant Wilson, Columbo's unwanted partner."},
"col-42809":{"s":"Eric Wagner is the young, careless owner of a professional football team, and Paul Hanlon is the sharp manager who really keeps it running. When Wagner threatens to make decisions that would damage everything Hanlon has built, the manager decides that the owner has no future.\n\nThe result looks like a sad accident in a swimming pool, and Hanlon has an alibi that places him in the stadium box during an important match. The lieutenant is a cheerful guest at the game, and he starts to wonder about a certain telephone call.","x":"The stadium box has a chiming clock, but there is no chime on the recorded phone call. This proves that Hanlon made it from somewhere else.","n":"Jeremy Kagan directed this episode, written by John T. Dugan. Robert Culp plays the manager and Dean Stockwell the victim. Valerie Harper and James Gregory also appear."},
"col-42810":{"s":"Columbo travels to London as a guest of Scotland Yard, expecting a quiet visit. Instead, he finds himself in the world of the theatre, where a famous husband-and-wife acting team have just finished a performance and a very serious accident has happened off stage.\n\nThe actors are experts at appearing natural, and they make a convincing pair of shocked and grieving friends. Columbo is a stranger in the country, a stranger to the theatre and an amateur at Shakespeare. That does not stop him from asking a lot of questions about a pearl, an umbrella and a very helpful butler.","x":"Columbo uses a trick with a pearl and an umbrella, taking aim with his skill at marbles. The result shakes the murderer into a confession.","n":"Richard Quine directed this episode, from a story by Levinson and Link and a teleplay by Jackson Gillis. Richard Basehart and Honor Blackman play the actors, and John Williams and Wilfrid Hyde-White also appear. It was the first of several episodes set abroad."},
"col-42811":{"s":"Nora Chandler was once among the greatest stars in Hollywood, and she still lives like one. But the roles have dried up, the money is less certain and her devoted home life hides an old secret. When a woman who knows too much appears, the former star decides to protect her reputation at any price.\n\nIt is a staged accident, and Nora plays the shocked friend beautifully. Columbo, one of her greatest admirers, is thrilled to meet her, which makes it awkward when his instincts keep telling him that something in her house is not right.","x":"Columbo notices a garden fountain that was bought just after Nora's husband vanished. He tricks her into believing the body has been found, and she hurries to the garden and confesses.","n":"Richard Quine directed this episode, written by Jackson Gillis. Anne Baxter plays the actress, and the famous costume designer Edith Head appears as herself. The episode plays on the real history of Hollywood's golden age."},
"col-42812":{"s":"Dr Barry Mayfield is a gifted and ambitious surgeon who shares a research project with a colleague. He is not prepared to share the credit, and he has found a way of removing his partner that will never look like murder. It will happen in the operating theatre, quietly, and the cause of death will not be obvious.\n\nBut a nurse has noticed something unusual, and she makes the mistake of mentioning it. Lieutenant Columbo arrives in a hospital he finds frightening, but he has a very clear view of how the doctor behaves under pressure.","x":"Mayfield swaps the sutures, so the police find nothing. Columbo later realises that he was never searched, returns and finds the missing sutures in the pocket of Mayfield's scrubs.","n":"Hy Averback directed this episode, written by Shirl Hendryx. Leonard Nimoy, famous as Spock in Star Trek, plays the surgeon. Anne Francis, Will Geer, Jared Martin and Nita Talbot are among the cast."},
"col-42813":{"s":"Emmett Clayton is a world-class chess player, famous for his cool nerves and long-range planning. The night before a big tournament, he decides that a rival stands in the way of a title, and he acts with the same calm precision he shows at the board.\n\nThe tournament goes on, with all the attention of the press and the public. Lieutenant Columbo is no chess player, but he is a very good observer of the people who play, and he begins to wonder whether the champion's behaviour is that of a man who has already made his next move.","x":"Columbo notices that the crime scene would have given the killer a clear signal if he could have heard it. He realises that the murderer must be hard of hearing, which points to Clayton.","n":"Edward M. Abroms directed this episode, with a teleplay by Jackson Gillis. Laurence Harvey plays the chess master and Jack Kruschen the victim. It is one of the few episodes in which Columbo is present at a death."},
"col-42814":{"s":"Dexter Paris is a smooth, popular television chef. His identical twin brother, Norman, is a stiff, serious banker. The two men could hardly be less alike, but they share a rich uncle, a large inheritance and a strong dislike of the way he controls the money.\n\nWhen the uncle is found electrocuted in his bath, both brothers are suspects, and both have apparently solid alibis. Lieutenant Columbo meets two men who look exactly the same and behave completely differently, and he has to find out which of them, if either, can be believed.","x":"Columbo works out that the crime needed two people, from the timing of the power cut and the physical effort of moving the body. When he presses them, one twin confesses while the other tells him to be quiet.","n":"Robert Butler directed this episode, with a story by Jackson Gillis, Levinson and Link and a teleplay by Steven Bochco. Martin Landau plays both twins. Paul Stewart, Julie Newmar and a young Dabney Coleman also appear."},
"col-42815":{"s":"Viveca Scott built a cosmetics empire on her glamour and her talent for selling hope. Her latest product, a wrinkle remover, could be the most successful she has ever launched, but its secret formula belongs to someone else: a chemist who was once her lover and now wants a share of the profits.\n\nViveca will not share anything. When the chemist is found dead, she is charming, elegant and helpful. Columbo arrives in her gleaming world of mirrors and beautiful models feeling out of place, but he has a keen eye for what does not belong.","x":"Columbo notices that both he and Viveca are itching, and traces it to poison ivy oil on a broken microscope slide. It proves that she handled the slide at the time of the murder.","n":"Jeannot Szwarc directed this episode, from a story by Myrna Bercovici and a teleplay by Jackson Gillis. Vera Miles plays the cosmetics boss and a young Martin Sheen plays the chemist. It is set in the glossy world of the beauty industry."},
"col-42816":{"s":"Adrian Carsini is a refined winemaker who loves his family vineyard and loves fine living even more. His gambling debts are growing, and his half-brother wants to sell the winery to a large company. For Adrian, that would mean losing the one thing that gives his life meaning.\n\nDuring a heatwave, a terrible accident happens in the wine cellar. Adrian is the perfect host, charming and courteous, and he welcomes Columbo with a glass of something special. The lieutenant, who knows nothing about wine, finds himself learning fast.","x":"Columbo borrows a rare bottle of 1945 port and has it served at dinner. Adrian realises the heat has spoilt his cellar and rushes to destroy the bottles, where Columbo is waiting.","n":"Leo Penn directed this episode, from a story by Larry Cohen and a teleplay by Stanley Ralph Ross. Donald Pleasence plays the winemaker, and Julie Harris plays his formidable secretary. The setting is a California wine estate."},
"col-42817":{"s":"Nelson Hayward is a handsome, popular candidate for the United States Senate, and his campaign manager, Harry Stone, knows exactly how much of that success is real. When Stone starts to use his power in ways that threaten the candidate's future, Hayward decides that his problem needs a permanent solution.\n\nThe murder is made to look like a failed attack on Hayward himself, perhaps by a hidden assassin. Instead of a quiet life as a politician's victim, Hayward has a lieutenant in a battered raincoat following him from one election event to the next.","x":"Hayward stages a fake shooting at his hotel with a firecracker. Columbo has already found the earlier bullet and the hole in the window, and the ballistics link them to the murder weapon.","n":"Boris Sagal directed this episode, from a story by Larry Cohen. Jackie Cooper plays the candidate and Ken Swofford the campaign manager. Katey Sagal, the director's daughter, has a small part."},
"col-42818":{"s":"Bart Keppel is an expert in motivational research who claims that he can influence what people want without their knowing it. He is also running a blackmail scheme, and one of his victims is about to become a problem. He chooses a cinema as the setting, where the audience is dark, quiet and looking at a screen.\n\nThe murder takes place during a film, and nobody seems to have seen a thing. The lieutenant, however, has a feeling that someone is trying to put ideas into his head, and he begins to study the cinema's equipment with unusual interest.","x":"Columbo splices a brief image of himself searching for evidence into Keppel's film. Keppel rushes to his office to retrieve a vital piece of equipment and is caught.","n":"Richard Quine directed this episode, written by Stephen J. Cannell. Robert Culp plays the researcher in his third villain role. It was a popular episode, and the idea of hidden images made it feel very modern for 1973."},
"col-42819":{"s":"Riley Greenleaf is a publisher with a famous name and a failing business. His star writer, Alan Mallory, writes the books that sell, but he is about to take them to another publisher. For Greenleaf, this is a disaster he is determined to prevent.\n\nHe hires a man who knows about explosives to deal with the problem, and then deals with that man himself. By the time Columbo arrives, the publisher has everything under control, or so he believes. The lieutenant is a reader of mysteries, and he enjoys a good plot, especially one with a twist at the end.","x":"Greenleaf gives Columbo an old synopsis to suggest that his accomplice plagiarised it. But Mallory had changed the ending, which the accomplice could not have known, and that exposes the lie.","n":"Robert Butler directed this episode, written by Peter S. Fischer. Jack Cassidy plays the publisher, his third and last role as a Columbo murderer in the early seasons. The crime writer Mickey Spillane plays the victim."},
"col-42820":{"s":"Dr Marshall Cahill is the proud director of a government think tank, a place where brilliant scientists are paid to imagine the future. His own son is one of them, and the father will do almost anything to protect the young man from a scandal that could destroy his career.\n\nWhen an elderly colleague threatens to reveal the truth, Cahill acts quickly and cleverly. He even makes use of one of the institute's own inventions to build his alibi. Columbo finds himself surrounded by geniuses, and he is rather pleased to be underestimated by every one of them.","x":"Columbo finds a clue that points away from the household and towards Cahill. Lacking firm proof, he sets up the son to look guilty, and the father confesses to protect him.","n":"Alf Kjellin directed this episode, from a story by Robert Specht and a teleplay credited to Steven Bochco, Dean Hargrove and Roland Kibbee. José Ferrer plays the director and Lew Ayres the victim. Robby the Robot, a film star of the 1950s, has a guest appearance."},
"col-42821":{"s":"Tommy Brown is a gospel singer adored by his fans, with a loyal public image and a wife who knows too much about his private life. She is determined to hold him to a past that he would prefer to forget, and he decides that the time has come to stop listening to her.\n\nThe plan involves a private plane, a flight over the desert and a very convenient accident. Tommy is a humble, grieving hero in the eyes of the press. Columbo is not so sure, and he starts to think about what a man would need to do to survive a crash.","x":"Columbo works out that Tommy must have used a hidden parachute. He notices that Tommy kept his rental car keys, then catches him returning at night to collect the parachute.","n":"Nicholas Colasanto directed this episode, from a story by Stanley Ralph Ross and a teleplay by David Rayfiel. The country singer Johnny Cash plays the gospel star, with Ida Lupino as his wife. It is one of the best-known episodes of the series."},
"col-42822":{"s":"Mark Halperin is a deputy police commissioner, respected and powerful. When his neighbour, a surgeon, accidentally kills his own wife and comes to him in panic, Halperin offers to help. He arranges a false alibi, makes the death look like the work of a burglar and expects loyalty in return.\n\nBut Halperin also has his own problem to solve, and soon the favour becomes a trap for the man he has helped. Columbo has to investigate his own superior, which makes the case unusually delicate, and the answer is nearer than anyone expects.","x":"Columbo feeds Halperin false details about a burglar. Halperin plants stolen jewellery at the burglar's address, but Columbo reveals that he is the one renting that flat, so only Halperin could have placed it there.","n":"Ben Gazzara directed this episode, written by Peter S. Fischer. Richard Kiley plays the deputy commissioner, with Michael McGuire and Rosemary Murphy in the cast. Gazzara went on to direct another episode of the series."},
"col-42823":{"s":"Milo Janus built a chain of fitness clubs from nothing, and he is proud of his body, his business and his name. One of his franchise owners, however, has found evidence that Janus has been cheating the very people who trusted him, and he is ready to speak.\n\nAn accident in a gym can happen to anyone, and Janus knows exactly how to arrange one. He welcomes Columbo with a handshake that is a little too strong. The lieutenant is no athlete, but he is observant, and he has a way of noticing what other people have missed.","x":"Columbo realises that the victim's trainers were tied by someone else. Only Janus knew the dead man had been dressed in workout clothes, so he must have dressed the body.","n":"Bernard Kowalski directed this episode, from a story by Larry Cohen and a teleplay by Peter S. Fischer. Robert Conrad plays the fitness boss and Philip Bruns the victim. The episode runs about 94 minutes, the longer format used in the fourth season."},
"col-42824":{"s":"Paul Galesko is a Pulitzer Prize-winning photographer who sees the world through a lens. His marriage has become a trap, and his wife has money and control that he resents. He decides to use the one skill he is sure no one can match: creating a convincing image of events that never took place.\n\nThe result looks like a kidnapping that went terribly wrong. Paul plays the horrified husband with an artist's flair. Columbo is a very poor photographer, but he has a great eye for the details that do not belong in a picture.","x":"Columbo shows Paul a mirror-reversed photograph and says the original was destroyed. Paul hurries to pick the right camera, which reveals that he knew exactly which one was used.","n":"Alf Kjellin directed this episode, written by Peter S. Fischer. Dick Van Dyke plays the photographer, a notable change from his comic roles. Joyce Van Patten appears as a nun, and Antoinette Bower and Don Gordon also take part."},
"col-42825":{"s":"Colonel Lyle Rumford is the disciplined, proud head of a military academy for boys. His whole life is built on tradition and order. When the school's board decides to change the rules, and to replace him, the colonel sees his world coming to an end.\n\nA ceremonial cannon, a quiet night and a carefully planned accident give him the chance he needs. Columbo is invited to stay at the academy for the investigation, and he soon finds himself in a bunk bed, learning about discipline, honour and the rules of military life.","x":"Rumford had hung cider to ferment, hidden where it could be seen only at sunrise from beside the cannon. He claimed to have been asleep, but his knowledge of the cider shows he was at the cannon that morning.","n":"Harvey Hart directed this episode, written by Howard Berk. Patrick McGoohan won the first of his two Emmy Awards for this role. Bruce Kirby and Bruno Kirby appear, and the episode was filmed at The Citadel military college in South Carolina."},
"col-42826":{"s":"Columbo and his wife are enjoying a rare holiday on a cruise ship bound for Acapulco. It is a chance to relax, to eat well and to forget about work. Then a used car dealer, Hayden Danziger, decides that his affair with the ship's lounge singer is no longer convenient.\n\nThe ship is a floating world with a limited number of suspects and a captain who would rather not have a scandal. Columbo tries to enjoy his holiday, but his curiosity is stronger than his wish to relax, and soon he is on the case.","x":"Danziger fires a shot in the engine room wearing gloves, then leaves them to be found. Columbo ignores the gunpowder test and finds Danziger's fingerprints inside the gloves.","n":"Ben Gazzara directed this episode, from a story by Jackson Gillis and William Driskill and a teleplay by Driskill. Robert Vaughn plays the car dealer, and Patrick Macnee plays the ship's captain. Dean Stockwell also appears."},
"col-42827":{"s":"Harold Van Wick is the head of an electronics company, and he loves gadgets. His house is full of cameras and recording equipment, a perfect picture of a modern, secure home. His mother-in-law, however, knows about his private life and intends to speak about it.\n\nVan Wick uses his technology to make the crime look like a robbery that took place after he had left for a party. The cameras do not lie, he says, and the evidence is on tape. Columbo, who is no friend of machines, takes a very careful look at what the cameras actually show.","x":"Van Wick's party invitation is visible on his desk in the recording, even though he said he had already left. He would have had to step over the body to retrieve it, so the timeline falls apart.","n":"Bernard Kowalski directed this episode, written by Booker Bradshaw and David P. Lewis. Oskar Werner plays the inventor, with Martha Scott and Gena Rowlands in the cast. At around 70 minutes, it is one of the shorter episodes."},
"col-42828":{"s":"Dr Mark Collier is a fashionable psychiatrist who specialises in hypnosis. He is also in love with a patient, whose husband stands in the way of their future together. After a violent argument, the husband is dead, and the doctor has a plan to persuade his lover to support his version of events.\n\nHis skill with the human mind makes the plan more dangerous than it first appears. Columbo is a man who prefers simple answers, and he is suspicious of anyone who seems to know exactly what another person is thinking.","x":"Columbo produces a witness in dark glasses who looks like a blind man the doctor nearly ran down. When the witness reads easily, Collier has revealed himself, and Columbo explains that the man is the blind man's brother.","n":"Harvey Hart directed this episode, written by Peter S. Fischer. George Hamilton plays the psychiatrist, with Lesley Ann Warren and Stephen Elliott in the cast. It runs about 70 minutes and ends the fourth season."},
"col-42829":{"s":"Grace Wheeler was once a famous dancer and film star, and she still lives in the glow of her old successes. Her husband, a doctor, will not finance her comeback, and she cannot bear the thought of being forgotten. One night, while her butler tells everyone she was watching one of her old films, her husband dies.\n\nIt looks like suicide, and Grace is a figure of dignified sorrow. Columbo has an enormous respect for the old stars, and he treats her very gently. But he is also a man who counts the minutes, and he starts to count the length of a film.","x":"The running time of the film does not match the time Grace says she spent in the screening room, even allowing for a repair. The ending is unusual and rather sad: Grace is not arrested.","n":"Harvey Hart directed this episode, written by William Driskill. Janet Leigh plays the former star, and Sam Jaffe plays her husband. The film clips shown in the episode come from Walking My Baby Back Home, a 1953 film in which Leigh really appeared."},
"col-42830":{"s":"Hassan Salah is the first secretary to the king of a small Middle Eastern country, a polished diplomat with an important visitor on the way. When a young colleague becomes a problem, Salah finds a way to remove him, and to make another man take the blame.\n\nThere is only one difficulty: Salah has diplomatic immunity, and no police officer can arrest him. He is smiling, formal and perfectly calm. Columbo has to find a way to bring justice to a man who is protected by the law, and he does it using patience and a great deal of courtesy.","x":"Columbo gets Salah to confess while the young king is listening in the next room. Salah gives up his immunity rather than be sent home, where he expects to be punished far more severely.","n":"Ted Post directed this episode, from a story by James Menzies and a teleplay by Lou Shaw. Héctor Elizondo and Sal Mineo have the main roles. The episode is notable for its political setting and for the performances of its two guest stars."},
"col-42831":{"s":"Nelson Brenner is a senior operative of the CIA, a man trained to deceive, to disappear and to leave no trace. At the moment he is working quietly as a writer of speeches, and an old colleague from his past turns up demanding money for an operation that never ended.\n\nBrenner deals with the problem the way an expert would, with an alibi built on a recording. He expects the lieutenant to be an easy opponent. But Columbo is not impressed by secrecy, and he has a talent for noticing the very small things that spies tend to forget.","x":"Brenner's alibi is a tape of a speech, but it refers to news that did not break until hours after the recording was supposedly made. Columbo uses this to prove the tape was not made when claimed.","n":"Patrick McGoohan both directed this episode and played the killer, from a script by William Driskill. Leslie Nielsen appears as the victim. McGoohan, the star of The Prisoner, slips in a knowing nod to that series."},
"col-42832":{"s":"Luis Montoya is a legendary bullfighter, a hero in his country and a figure of great pride. In the ring, one terrible moment of fear has changed everything, and his bookkeeper is the only man who knows the truth. When the bookkeeper is found dead in a bullring, it looks as if the animal has killed him.\n\nColumbo is in Mexico for a weekend away, and he is asked to help a local police chief. He knows nothing about bullfighting, but he knows a lot about people, and he is curious about a man who seems so fearless and yet so tense.","x":"Columbo uses the condition of the cape to establish the time of death, which leaves Montoya without an alibi. He then lures the bullfighter into the ring, where he freezes again, this time in front of witnesses.","n":"Ted Post directed this episode, written by Brad Radnitz. Ricardo Montalbán plays the bullfighter. The Mexican director Emilio Fernández has a small role, and film footage from the 1947 film Fiesta is used."},
"col-42833":{"s":"The Great Santini is a stage magician, expert in escapes, misdirection and clever deceptions. His business manager, however, has been blackmailing him, and Santini has decided that the ultimate escape is one from his problem. During a famous water-tank act, in front of a full theatre, he performs a trick no one will see.\n\nThe murder looks like the work of a hired killer, and Santini is the perfect witness. Columbo has come to the theatre to admire the magic, but he has spotted that a good magician and a good criminal have a great deal in common.","x":"Columbo recovers the used carbon ribbon from the victim's typewriter, which still holds the letter he was writing to the authorities. It reveals the motive and breaks the illusion.","n":"Harvey Hart directed this episode, written by Michael Sloan. Jack Cassidy plays the magician, his third and last appearance as a Columbo killer. Bob Dishy returns as Sergeant Wilson, here named John."},
"col-42834":{"s":"Commodore Swanson is a veteran sailor and the founder of a successful shipbuilding company, and he is planning to sell the business. That night he is found dead on his yacht. His son-in-law, Charles Clay, who runs the company, is the obvious suspect, and Columbo is determined to find out what really happened.\n\nThe case brings the lieutenant to a sunny marina, where everyone seems to have a good reason to be unhappy. It also brings him into contact with two police colleagues, and the case turns into something more like a comedy than a normal murder investigation.","x":"Columbo holds up the Commodore's broken pocket watch and claims it is still ticking. Only the real killer would know it had stopped, and Swanny Swanson is the one who contradicts him.","n":"Patrick McGoohan directed this episode, written by Jackson Gillis. It breaks the series' normal format, with a lighter tone and a different kind of investigation. Fred Draper, John Dehner and Robert Vaughn are in the cast."},
"col-42835":{"s":"Ward Fowler is the star of a popular television series, in which he plays a brilliant detective. In real life he is vain, comfortable and afraid. His producer knows a secret from his past and has started to use it against him, and the actor decides that a little acting of his own may solve the problem.\n\nHe stages a robbery, hides behind a mask and makes sure that the evidence points in a different direction. Columbo, a big fan of the show, is delighted to visit the studio, but he has noticed that real detectives do not have scripts, and real criminals do not either.","x":"The gun carries no fingerprints, but Fowler's prints are on the live rounds still in the chamber. That is the evidence that links him to the weapon.","n":"Bernard Kowalski directed this episode, from a story by Henry Garson and a teleplay by Peter Feibleman and Lou Shaw. William Shatner plays the actor, and Shera Danese, later Peter Falk's wife, has a small role. The mechanical shark from Jaws makes a cameo."},
"col-42836":{"s":"Ruth Lytton is the strong-minded head of a family museum that has been in the family for generations. Money is short, and her brother wants to close the whole business. For Ruth, the museum is more than a job, and she will not let it go.\n\nShe arranges a robbery with a former criminal as her helper, then deals with both the helper and her brother, and prepares to blame the family's young niece. Columbo wanders among the museum's treasures with great interest, and he takes a particular liking to an old belt buckle.","x":"Ruth plants an ancient belt buckle in her niece's room. When the niece uses it as an ashtray, Columbo sees that she cannot know its value and cannot be the thief. Ruth then confesses to avoid an old crime being investigated.","n":"Robert Douglas directed this episode, from a story by Lawrence Vail and a teleplay by Peter Feibleman. Joyce Van Patten plays the museum director, and Celeste Holm and Jeannie Berlin appear. Feibleman also plays one of the characters."},
"col-42837":{"s":"Oliver Brandt is a member of the Sigma Society, a club open only to people with very high intelligence, and he is proud of his mind. He is also an accountant who has been stealing from his own firm to keep his wife happy. When his business partner discovers the secret, Brandt applies his intelligence to making the problem disappear.\n\nHe constructs an alibi that seems perfect, with a time-plan worthy of a puzzle book. Columbo enters a world of very clever people and finds himself slightly embarrassed by his own lack of IQ points, which is exactly what he wants his suspect to think.","x":"Columbo offers a deliberately ridiculous explanation of the timing of the shots. Brandt, offended by such a poor theory, demonstrates how it was really done, and in doing so gives himself away.","n":"Sam Wanamaker directed this episode, written by Robert Malcolm Young. Theodore Bikel plays the accountant, and Jamie Lee Curtis has a small role as a waitress. This was the last episode shown under the original NBC Mystery Movie banner."},
"col-42838":{"s":"Abigail Mitchell is a famous writer of mystery novels, an elderly lady with a sharp mind and a taste for clever plots. She believes that her niece's husband caused the girl's death, and she has decided that the law is too slow to deal with him. Her house contains a very special room that she designed herself.\n\nShe invites the young man to stay, and the plan is simple, and very well thought out. Columbo is a great admirer of her books, and he is delighted to meet her. But he has realised that a writer who knows how to plan a murder may also know how to carry one out.","x":"Before he died, the victim tore a strip from the title page of her manuscript and darkened some letters so that it read as an accusation. He hid it in the safe's light socket, where Columbo found it.","n":"James Frawley directed this episode, from a story by Gene Thompson and a teleplay by Thompson and Paul Tuckahoe. Ruth Gordon, then aged 81, plays the writer and is the oldest killer in the series. Mariette Hartley plays her assistant."},
"col-42839":{"s":"Paul Gerard is a restaurant critic whose opinion can make or break any chef in the city. His fame, his elegant manner and his sophisticated taste hide a nasty secret: he has been taking money from restaurant owners in exchange for good reviews. One of them is no longer willing to pay.\n\nThe murder takes place in a very fine restaurant, with a very rare and very exotic poison. Columbo, who is happier with chilli and a bowl of simple food, is invited to dine with the critic, and he does his best to appear impressed.","x":"Columbo works out that the poison was applied through the needle of the bottle opener. He sets a trap in which Gerard tries the same method on him, which provides the evidence.","n":"Jonathan Demme, later famous as a film director, directed this episode in his first television job. Robert Van Scoyk's teleplay won an Edgar Award. Louis Jourdan plays the critic, and Shera Danese appears as his assistant."},
"col-42840":{"s":"Kay Freestone is an ambitious television executive who has given everything to her career, and who expected her lover, the head of the network, to give her the promotion she deserved. When he gives the job to someone else, her disappointment turns into something far more dangerous.\n\nThe murder takes place during a private screening in his office, and it relies on one small piece of timing. Kay is cool, professional and very helpful. Columbo is not an expert in television, but he knows how to pick a lock, and he knows when someone is covering up for someone else.","x":"Kay hides the gun in an elevator ceiling. Columbo finds the real weapon and replaces it with a replica; when she retrieves and disposes of it, her action proves her guilt.","n":"James Frawley directed this episode, written by Robert Blees. Trish Van Devere plays the executive, and Laurence Luckinbill and Patrick O'Neal appear. It was the last episode featuring Mike Lally, a regular who appeared in many episodes."},
"col-42841":{"s":"Dr Eric Mason is a behavioural psychologist with an elegant home, a very bright mind and two beautifully trained Dobermans. His wife died in strange circumstances, and he believes his friend Charlie Hunter was involved with her. For Mason, revenge is a problem of training and conditioning.\n\nHe has taught the dogs to respond to a certain word, spoken over the telephone. He is a polite man, but cold, with a laboratory-like view of life. Columbo, who is a little afraid of dogs, finds himself in a house where an ordinary phone call may prove fatal.","x":"Columbo secretly records Mason saying the trigger word and plays it back to the dogs. He then provokes Mason into giving the command, but the dogs, who have been retrained, simply play with him.","n":"James Frawley directed this episode, from a story by Anthony Lawrence and a teleplay by Tom Lazarus. Nicol Williamson plays the psychologist, and a very young Kim Cattrall and Ed Begley Jr. have small roles."},
"col-42842":{"s":"Joe Devlin is an Irish poet, a great talker and a popular figure in Los Angeles. He is also secretly involved in raising money and moving weapons for a cause back home. When he learns that a middleman in a weapons deal plans to betray him, he acts decisively.\n\nThe lieutenant is investigating a murder, but the case soon takes him into a world of secrets, politics and loyalty. Devlin is eloquent, friendly and always ready with a story and a drink. Columbo listens carefully to every word, and he has a very good memory for what people say.","x":"A whiskey bottle at the scene carries marks from Devlin's diamond ring. Columbo also stops the smuggling by recognising the shipping company's colours on a tugboat carrying the weapons.","n":"Leo Penn directed this episode, written by Howard Berk. Clive Revill plays the poet. It was the final Columbo episode shown on NBC, and its closing line comes from a speech by the Irish leader Charles Stewart Parnell."},
"col-42843":{"s":"Elliott Blake is a charismatic psychic who has persuaded military intelligence officers that he can see things at a distance. His great enemy is Max Dyson, a magician who makes a living exposing frauds, and who knows far more about Blake than Blake would like. The two men have a shared and difficult past.\n\nWhen Dyson prepares a famous trick involving a guillotine, Blake sees his opportunity. The lieutenant arrives with a great deal of curiosity about psychic powers, and with a very low opinion of his own. He promises Blake that he will be watching him very closely indeed.","x":"Columbo challenges Blake, who tries to kill him with the guillotine and confesses as he pulls the plug. But Columbo had secretly rigged the machine, so he is unharmed.","n":"Leo Penn directed this episode, written by William Read Woodfield. Anthony Andrews plays the psychic and Anthony Zerbe the magician, who is based on the real sceptic James Randi. It was the first episode shown on ABC."},
"col-42844":{"s":"Alex Brady is a famous film director, a man with a great name and a dark secret from the early days of his career. Years ago, a young man died during one of his first films, and the death was ruled an accident. Now the victim's brother has found some film that could change everything.\n\nBrady is determined that no one should see it. The studio is full of lights, props and actors, and the lieutenant finds himself in a world where nothing is quite what it seems. He also discovers that he does not enjoy being on a moving camera platform.","x":"Brady hires two actresses to perform a staged conversation, but Columbo notices one is dressed as a nurse for no reason. He then uses undercover officers to overhear Brady offering a bribe.","n":"James Frawley directed this episode, written by Richard Alan Simmons. Fisher Stevens plays the director, and Molly Hagan plays his girlfriend. The episode is a tribute to the golden age of Hollywood filmmaking."},
"col-42845":{"s":"Joan Allenby is a popular sex therapist with a bestselling book and a television show, and her private life is built on trust. When she learns that her lover and business partner has betrayed her, her calm professional manner changes into something different. She devises a plan that is much more cunning than he expects.\n\nShe becomes someone else for one evening, with a new look and a new name, and the evidence points to a mysterious woman. Columbo is invited to appear on her show, and he is rather embarrassed about the subject, but he is very good at listening.","x":"Columbo finds a price tag from Allenby's new coat in the rubbish, and a shop assistant recognises her from her book photograph. A police officer dressed as the mysterious woman also provokes a revealing silence.","n":"James Frawley directed this episode, written by Jerry Ludwig. Lindsay Crouse plays the therapist and Stephen Macht the victim. The title is a play on the movie title Sex and the Single Girl."},
"col-42846":{"s":"Colonel Frank Brailie runs a private military school and think tank, a place of discipline, loyalty and secret money. He has been taking funds from the foundation for his own use, and his affair with the general's wife is no less dangerous. When a sergeant major discovers the truth and demands payment, Brailie acts.\n\nThe death occurs during a night exercise and looks like an accident, and a toy battle of Gettysburg gives him an alibi. The lieutenant, who dislikes weapons, is an unusual visitor, and he asks questions that no one has thought of before.","x":"Columbo notices that a box labelled as books was too small to hold them. It really contained the toy soldiers, and Brailie had swapped the labels, which destroys his alibi.","n":"Sam Wanamaker directed this episode, written by Sy Salkowitz. Robert Foxworth plays the colonel and Andy Romano the victim. It is the last of the four episodes shown in the 1989 season."},
"col-42847":{"s":"Max Barsini is an internationally famous painter, a man of great talent, great ego and great bad temper. His ex-wife plans to reveal a secret that could ruin his reputation, and she has been in touch with a journalist. When she is found drowned, it looks like an accident, and Barsini has an alibi: he was painting in a bar.\n\nThe lieutenant arrives at the artist's house and, to his surprise, is asked to sit for a portrait. Columbo is flattered but also watchful, and as he sits, he studies the studio, the paints and the painter.","x":"A mark on the dead woman's face is not make-up but a special colour that Barsini mixes only for himself. It is also found on the cloth used to subdue her.","n":"James Frawley directed this episode, written by Robert Sherman. Patrick Bauchau plays the painter, and Fionnula Flanagan plays his ex-wife. The episode, at about 88 minutes, was part of the new ABC series."},
"col-42848":{"s":"Dian Hunter is the publisher of a men's magazine, and she has decided to sell her share of the company to a media tycoon. Her business partner and lover, Sean Brantley, is strongly opposed. A short time later, she appears to disappear on a flight to London, and the police are asked to investigate.\n\nThe lieutenant, who has been involved in an earlier case with the magazine's staff, has to decide whether anyone has really vanished. Sean is a charming, confident man who knows that Columbo has already searched the house once. He also has a very clever idea about where to hide a problem.","x":"Columbo notices that a fur coat is missing while the others are in storage, and deduces the body is in the missing bag. He calls the pager on her wrist, which rings behind a new section of wall.","n":"Daryl Duke directed this episode, written by William Read Woodfield. Ian Buchanan plays the partner and Deidre Hall the missing publisher. The episode is a witty play on the story of the boy who cried wolf."},
"col-42849":{"s":"Oscar Finch is a respected lawyer with big political dreams. A powerful man from his past now threatens to reveal a favour that could end Finch's career. The meeting between the two men ends in a death, and Finch dresses it up as a suicide with great care.\n\nThe lieutenant is called in and quickly decides that the apparent suicide leaves a few loose ends. He does not accuse anyone, but he asks a number of rather awkward questions, and he is especially interested in the way a man treats his cigars and his food.","x":"Columbo realises the killer bit into a block of cheese at the scene. He matches the bite to Finch's teeth using chewing gum that Finch had thrown away.","n":"Patrick McGoohan directed this episode and plays Finch, from a script by Jeffrey Bloom. It earned McGoohan his second Emmy Award, after his win for By Dawn's Early Light. Louis Zorich plays the victim."},
"col-42850":{"s":"The episode opens in a way no one expects: at the funeral of Mrs Columbo. A young woman, Vivian Dimitri, believes that the lieutenant destroyed her husband's life, and she has decided that he should know what loss feels like. She has already killed her boss, and she plans a second crime, much closer to home.\n\nTold partly in flashback, the story shows a very different Columbo, a man in deep sorrow. But the lieutenant has not forgotten how to be a detective, and he has a surprise of his own.","x":"Columbo and the police fake Mrs Columbo's death, and Columbo pretends to die of poison while Dimitri confesses to a recording in another house. The poison had been replaced with an ordinary jar of marmalade.","n":"Vincent McEveety directed this episode, written by Peter S. Fischer. Helen Shaver plays the vengeful widow, and Roscoe Lee Browne appears as her psychiatrist. It was shown as part of the ABC Saturday Mystery series."},
"col-42851":{"s":"Wesley Corman is the junior partner in a successful dental practice, owned by his father-in-law. He is a gambler, a poor husband and a man who is about to lose everything. His wife has taken a lover and plans to leave him, and her family is turning against him.\n\nCorman decides that there is a neat solution, and a dentist's tools are well suited to the job. He is careful, calm and charming. Columbo, who hates going to the dentist, arrives to investigate, and soon begins to wonder about a very small piece of dental work.","x":"Columbo bluffs that an exhumation will show a blue stain on a tooth under the crown, caused by a reaction with digitalis. Corman confesses before the examination, although the reaction does not exist.","n":"Alan J. Levi directed this episode, written by Steven Bochco, who used a similar plot in a 1977 episode of McMillan and Wife. James Read plays the dentist, and Nancy Walker appears as herself."},
"col-42852":{"s":"Theresa Goren is a successful writer of romantic novels, and her private life is just as dramatic as her books. Her young boyfriend, Wayne Jennings, is ambitious, charming and a little too fond of other women. Theresa is found shot dead in her house in Malibu, and the police begin to investigate.\n\nColumbo arrives at a beautiful beach home and meets a family in turmoil. As always, he starts with very small questions, about phone calls, clothes and the order in which things happened. This case has more than one twist, and he proceeds slowly.","x":"Columbo notices that the dead woman's underwear was put on backwards, something a woman would not do. This suggests that a man dressed her, and it points to her boyfriend.","n":"Walter Grauman directed this episode, written by Jackson Gillis. Andrew Stevens plays the boyfriend and Janet Margolin the novelist. The episode departs from the usual format by revealing an earlier fatal shot after the killer has fired."},
"col-42853":{"s":"Columbo is invited to speak to a class of criminology students at a university. It is a pleasant change from the usual routine, and he is happy to share a few lessons from his long experience. But two of the students are not as innocent as they look, and their professor soon turns up dead.\n\nThe young men are intelligent, sure of themselves and convinced they can outsmart the lieutenant. They offer him leads, theories and a convenient story about the underworld. Columbo thanks them politely, and appears to follow every suggestion.","x":"Columbo lets the students overhear a description of a suspect's car. They plant the murder weapon in it, but it is actually his wife's car, which only the killers could have been told about.","n":"E. W. Swackhamer directed this episode, from a story by Jeffrey Bloom and Frederick King Keller and a teleplay by Bloom. Robert Culp appears as the father of one of the killers, the only one of his four roles in which he is not the murderer."},
"col-42854":{"s":"Wade Anders is the host of a popular television programme about crime prevention, and he presents himself as a great expert on security. A rival newscaster has found an old adult video that could ruin him, and Anders sees no alternative to silence.\n\nHe carries out the plan during the working day, with a perfect alibi built from the cameras in his own office. The result looks like a natural death. The lieutenant, who is not very skilled with modern technology, meets a man who is very proud of his own.","x":"Columbo notices that bushes outside the office look freshly trimmed in the morning but fully grown by the evening, showing the security tape was faked. The victim's dog also scratched the killer's car.","n":"Daryl Duke directed this episode, written by Sonia Wolf, Patricia Ford and April Raynell. George Hamilton, who had played a murderer in an earlier episode, stars as Anders."},
"col-42855":{"s":"Hugh Creighton is one of the best-known defence lawyers in the country, and he is as talented in court as he is arrogant. His lover is a rock star who has been blackmailing him, and when she is found dead at her beach house, Creighton is ready with a story.\n\nBut there is a second problem: a colleague, a young woman in his firm, knows more than she should. She wants a partnership and is prepared to wait for it. Columbo, who is not a music fan, meets two very clever opponents, and he treats each of them with perfect politeness.","x":"Shadows in a speeding-ticket photograph show that the face of the driver was a flat mask, so the associate drove Creighton's car. Berries and droppings in the wiper wells show where her car was parked.","n":"Alan J. Levi directed this episode, written by William Read Woodfield. Dabney Coleman, who had a small role in an earlier season, plays the lawyer. The episode is set in the world of showbusiness and law."},
"col-42856":{"s":"Freddie Brower is a struggling photographer with a failing marriage and a very lucky ticket. He has won thirty million dollars in the lottery, and he does not want his wife to share it before the divorce is final. He asks his uncle Leon to claim the prize for him and to hand over the money later.\n\nUncle Leon, however, has ideas of his own. He is a jeweller with big debts and a taste for expensive things, and a Halloween party gives him a good chance. Columbo arrives at the party to find a house full of costumes, and a very curious chimpanzee.","x":"A chimpanzee that handled shiny objects left prints on a medallion from the jeweller's costume, placing him at the scene. Columbo's confrontation leads the killer to implicate the wife.","n":"Vincent McEveety directed this episode, written by Jeffrey Bloom. Rip Torn plays the uncle and Betsy Palmer his wife. It was the first episode of the eleventh season, shown in December 1991."},
"col-42857":{"s":"Columbo is attending the wedding of his nephew, a young police officer with a bright future. It is a happy day, with music, cake and family. But as the couple prepares to leave for their honeymoon, the bride disappears, and the day turns from joy to fear.\n\nColumbo has to act quickly, but the case is not like his others. There is no obvious murder, no clever alibi and no time to wait. He suspects that his nephew has enemies in the police force, and the search leads him through streets and buildings where nothing is simple.","x":"The kidnapper is a dangerous, sadistic criminal, and Columbo races to find the bride before it is too late. This is the only episode in the series without a murder and without a meeting between Columbo and the criminal at the start.","n":"Alan J. Levi directed this episode, from a story by Ed McBain with a teleplay by Robert Van Scoyk. Daniel McDonald and Joanna Going play the kidnapper and the bride. The story is based on a McBain novel, although it is not credited."},
"col-42858":{"s":"Harold McCain loves to gamble, and he usually loses. His debts are enormous, and his only hope is his uncle, Big Fred McCain, a millionaire who owns a football team and who refuses to give him a single dollar. Harold decides that waiting for an inheritance is no longer an option.\n\nThe plan involves a car, a quiet street and a moment of carefully chosen timing. But things do not go as Harold intended, and Columbo, who happens to be nearby, sees more than he should. The lieutenant has to piece together a story full of strange coincidences.","x":"Television footage shows Harold closing his eyes just before the explosion, suggesting he knew it was coming. Hairs from a fresh haircut in his hat, found in the widow's house, prove he was there. She is arrested.","n":"Vincent McEveety directed this episode, written by Jackson Gillis, his last script for the series. Tyne Daly and Greg Evigan play the nephew's wife and the nephew, and Steve Forrest the uncle. Columbo witnesses the death, which is rare."},
"col-42859":{"s":"Lauren Staton and Lisa Martin are two wealthy, elegant women who share a problem: the same man. Nick Franco has been unfaithful to both of them, and together they plan an ending that looks like a burglary gone wrong. Each has a solid alibi, and each has done her part in the plan.\n\nColumbo arrives at the apartment and finds a puzzle with two possible solutions. One of the women is charming, thoughtful and clearly interested in the lieutenant. He is flattered but watchful, and he knows that good manners can hide a great deal.","x":"Two photographs show the same antique chair, linking the two women. Columbo confronts one behind a one-way mirror, and the other confesses to protect her; Columbo then destroys the photographic evidence.","n":"Vincent McEveety directed this episode, which Peter Falk himself wrote. Faye Dunaway, who played the killer, won an Emmy for her performance. It is the only episode of the series written by Falk."},
"col-42860":{"s":"Fielding Chase is a famous radio host with an enormous voice and an enormous ego. He has controlled his daughter's life for years, and he cannot accept that a member of his staff has encouraged her to become a writer in New York. For Chase, this is a betrayal.\n\nHe arranges for the man to be shot while the two are speaking on the phone, and a recording on his answering machine seems to prove he was not there. His alibi is clever, his explanation is smooth and everyone seems satisfied. Columbo, a man with no taste for fancy equipment, listens very carefully.","x":"Chase claims he called the emergency services from his car on a mountain road, but that stretch of road had no mobile coverage. His alibi collapses.","n":"Dennis Dugan directed this episode, written by Peter S. Fischer. William Shatner plays the radio host, a second Columbo role after his earlier appearance as a television detective. It marked a return to the classic format."},
"col-42861":{"s":"A group of men and women are being killed, one after another, and each of them held a small piece of a photograph. The photograph is the key to four million dollars from an old robbery, and someone is determined to collect all the pieces.\n\nAn insurance investigator asks for Columbo's help, and the lieutenant goes undercover to track down the missing fragments. It is a story with a different shape from most, built on a jigsaw puzzle and a growing list of victims. Columbo has to decide whom to trust, and who is playing a part.","x":"The insurance investigator is the culprit, and Columbo breaks his alibi with a fingerprint on a coin in a parking meter near one of the murder scenes.","n":"Vincent McEveety directed this episode, from a story by Ed McBain and a teleplay by Gerry Day. Based on McBain's novel Jigsaw, it is unusual in that the villain's identity is not revealed until the end. Tyne Daly appears as a different character."},
"col-42862":{"s":"Graham McVeigh owns a successful ranch, and he has a brother who is a gambler with enormous debts. When the brother stands in the way of Graham's plans, Graham arranges to remove him, and to put the blame on a bookmaker with links to organised crime. He then deals with the bookmaker, claiming self-defence.\n\nNow he has problems on two sides: the lieutenant, who has doubts about his story, and the bookie's powerful boss, who wants justice of his own. Columbo finds himself caught between a killer and the underworld, and he has to be careful.","x":"Columbo stages an ambush at a dinner. Believing his life is in danger, the killer confesses, and Columbo reveals that it was a set-up.","n":"Vincent McEveety directed this episode, written by Lawrence Vail. George Wendt, best known as Norm in Cheers, plays the rancher, and Rod Steiger appears as the crime boss."},
"col-42863":{"s":"A beautiful woman and her boyfriend, an expert in forensic science, have a plan to get rid of her very rich husband. They do not intend to kill him themselves: they want him to be arrested for a murder that someone else has committed, and to leave the fortune to her.\n\nThe forensic expert knows exactly how the police work, and he has prepared the evidence with care. Columbo meets a very polished pair, and he appears to be impressed by science. He also keeps his eyes open for the places where science is not as perfect as it seems.","x":"At a restaurant, the forensic expert hands over a sweetener and opens a car door for the woman, showing they know each other better than they claim. A wedding photograph also reveals planted cat hair.","n":"Vincent McEveety directed this episode, written by Charles Kipps. David Rasche and Shera Danese play the couple, in her final appearance in the series. It was shown as a 25th anniversary special."},
"col-42864":{"s":"Verity Chandler is a famous Hollywood gossip columnist, and her sharp tongue has made her many enemies. One of them is a respected funeral director with a very old secret. When Verity threatens to reveal it, the man decides to put an end to her career, and her life.\n\nHe hides the body in a coffin, where nobody will think to look, and he plans a very quiet and dignified funeral. Columbo arrives at a scene of flowers, music and polite condolences, and discovers that death can be an expensive business. He takes a particular interest in the arrangements.","x":"A piece of shrapnel from the dead woman's old war wound survived the cremation and was found in the urn, exposing the switch.","n":"Patrick McGoohan directed this episode and plays the funeral director, his fourth role as a Columbo killer. Jeffrey Hatcher wrote the teleplay, and Rue McClanahan plays the victim."},
"col-42865":{"s":"Findlay Crawford is a famous film composer and conductor, with an Oscar to his name and a talent for collecting praise. The truth is that the music has been written for years by a young, gifted musician who hides in the background. When the young man decides to claim the credit, the composer sees his career in danger.\n\nThe murder is staged during a concert, in a big hall, with hundreds of people nearby. Columbo has a taste for simple tunes, but he arrives with great respect for the musicians, and he has an ear for what does not fit.","x":"Dried blood on the victim's hand reveals he was given a sedative, which explains the lack of screams. Oversized shoes and a missing house key point to the killer.","n":"Patrick McGoohan directed this episode, from a story by Jeffrey Cava, and he co-wrote the teleplay with Cava. Billy Connolly plays the composer. It was produced in 1999 but not shown until 2001."},
"col-42866":{"s":"A tabloid reporter has been found dead, and the death looks like suicide. But the dead man was a photographer who made his living from other people's secrets, and Columbo suspects that someone had very good reasons to want him silent.\n\nThe investigation takes the lieutenant into the world of Los Angeles nightclubs and raves, with loud music, bright lights and young people dancing until dawn. Columbo is a very long way from his comfort zone, but he is curious, patient and, as always, very polite to everyone.","x":"A koi tank in the club's dance floor has fewer fish and is shallower than the others. Ground-penetrating radar reveals the body beneath it.","n":"Jeffrey Reiner directed this episode, written by Michael Alaimo. Matthew Rhys plays the club promoter. It was the final episode of the series, and the club scenes use two tracks from The Crystal Method's album Tweekend."}
});
